# name: test/sql/languages/bash_variable_named_command.test
# description: A bash command's name is the text as written -- a variable, a string, or a compound
# group: [languages]

# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# `$verbose && echo "on"` and `if $DEBUG; then` are the idiomatic bash boolean:
# a variable holding `true` or `false`, used in command position. The command
# that actually runs is the variable's RUNTIME value, so there is no statically
# knowable command name.
#
# THE PREVIOUS ANSWER WAS AN EMPTY NAME, ON PURPOSE. languages/bash/bash_adapter.cpp
# returned "" for this shape, with the reasoning that naming it would record the
# VARIABLE rather than the command, and that python (`fns[k]()`) and javascript
# (`obj[k]()`) both return "" for a dynamic callee -- so an empty name was the
# house answer across three languages.
#
# THE CITATION WAS THE MISTAKE, not the convention. The house rule is that a
# callee with NO NAME IN THE SOURCE gets an empty name. `$cmd` has one. Teague
# made the distinction on 2026-11-18, and `$cmd` differs from `fns[k]()` in four
# ways:
#
#   1. `$cmd` names ONE variable, written right there. `fns[k]` is a compound
#      expression -- two variables and a subscript -- and no identifier in it
#      names the callee. There is nothing to write down.
#   2. Bash has no first-class functions and no function references, so
#      expansion in command position is THE way to parameterize a command. In
#      python `fns[k]()` is one dispatch idiom among many.
#   3. The written form IS the interface. `$CC`, `$MAKE` and `$PYTHON` in a
#      build script are the meaningful unit, and more useful to a reader than
#      whatever they resolved to on one machine.
#   4. `$cmd` often resolves -- an assignment is frequently visible in the same
#      file. `fns[k]` essentially never does.
#
# So naming `$verbose` is that rule applied correctly, not an exception to it.
# "The name as written" is also the convention for kotlin's parent types and
# jsx's `svg:circle`.
#
# THE RULE WIDENED AGAIN ON 2026-11-18, AND THIS FILE'S FIRST VERSION HAD THE
# BOUNDARY WRONG. It argued that a STRING in command position stays unnamed,
# because a string may hold a compound like `"$prefix/bin/$tool"` and no single
# identifier in it names the callee. Teague's answer: "that command's name is
# the whole string: $prefix/bin/$tool". Looking for ONE identifier was the
# mistake -- the name is the whole written form, exactly as it is for `${CMD}`.
#
# SO THE NAME IS THE TEXT OF THE `command_name` NODE, with one exception. The
# quote characters come off, because they are shell syntax and not part of the
# name: `"$cmd"` and `$cmd` name the same command and must join to each other,
# and `'ls'` is the command `ls`. The unquoted compound `$prefix/bin/$tool` is a
# `concatenation` node rather than a string, and takes the same answer -- which
# is the form Teague wrote.
#
# WHAT THE NAME MEANS IN THESE CASES, which is the point of keeping the `$`:
# it is AN INDICATION OF AN UNEVALUATED NAME (Teague, 2026-11-18). The command
# that runs is the value, and the value is not knowable here, so the name
# records the expression that will produce it. A consumer can tell the two
# apart by the leading `$`: `date` ran `date`, and `$CC` ran whatever `$CC`
# held.
#
# SO THE ANSWER IS DELIBERATELY IMPERFECT, and that is accepted rather than
# overlooked. These are bashisms. Row 5 is the clearest case: an ANSI-C string
# keeps its `$'...'` spelling, because the text starts with `$` and only a
# MATCHED outer pair is stripped. `ansi` is arguable, and nothing in the corpus
# exercises the shape, so no fixture measured it either way until this row.
# Teague's reading: imperfect is fine at this layer, and the refinement belongs
# in the semantic type refinements rather than in this string. Pinned here
# because an undecided edge is how this file's guard control got voided twice in
# one day.
#
# THE ONE EXCEPTION IS A COMMAND SUBSTITUTION, which has no single callee at all
# (#91). `$(which ls) -l` runs whatever `which ls` printed, and the inner
# command already carries `which` on its own row. A substitution inside a string
# (`"$(which ls)" -l`) is the same fact wearing quotes, so it is unnamed too.
#
# HOW TO READ A FAILURE HERE
#
#   Section 1 fails -> ordinary command naming broke. `date +%s` is the #140
#                      regression guard: FIND_IDENTIFIER once named the first
#                      ARGUMENT, so `date +%s` came out as `+%s`.
#   Section 2 fails -> the written forms stopped being named as written.
#   Section 3 fails -> the boundary moved. Something is now naming a command
#                      substitution, which has no single callee to name.
#   Section 4 fails -> the real fixture regressed, or the conformance corpus
#                      stopped exercising this shape.

require sitting_duck

# =============================================================================
# 1. Ordinary commands still name correctly, including #140's guard
# =============================================================================
# First, because every assertion below reads the same column. `date +%s` is here
# because naming the first argument is the specific wrong answer this adapter
# code was written to stop.

query TT
SELECT 'echo hi' AS src, name FROM parse_ast('echo hi', 'bash') WHERE type = 'command'
UNION ALL
SELECT 'date +%s', name FROM parse_ast('date +%s', 'bash') WHERE type = 'command'
ORDER BY src;
----
date +%s	date
echo hi	echo

# =============================================================================
# 2. The command name is the text AS WRITTEN
# =============================================================================
# Both expansion spellings: `$VAR` is a simple_expansion, `${VAR}` is an
# expansion. The leading `$` is kept deliberately -- it is what tells a consumer
# the callee is dynamic.

query TT
SELECT '$verbose' AS src, name FROM parse_ast('$verbose && echo x', 'bash')
  WHERE type = 'command' AND name LIKE '$%'
UNION ALL
SELECT '$DEBUG', name FROM parse_ast('if $DEBUG; then :; fi', 'bash')
  WHERE type = 'command' AND name LIKE '$%'
UNION ALL
SELECT '${CMD}', name FROM parse_ast('${CMD} arg', 'bash')
  WHERE type = 'command' AND name LIKE '$%'
ORDER BY src;
----
$DEBUG	$DEBUG
$verbose	$verbose
${CMD}	${CMD}

# A STRING, a RAW STRING and an unquoted COMPOUND take the same answer, with the
# quote characters removed. Labels are numbered so the sort order is readable
# rather than a byte-order puzzle.
query TT
SELECT '1 "$cmd"' AS src, name FROM parse_ast('"$cmd" arg', 'bash') WHERE type = 'command'
UNION ALL
SELECT '2 ''ls''', name FROM parse_ast('''ls'' -l', 'bash') WHERE type = 'command'
UNION ALL
SELECT '3 $prefix/bin/$tool', name FROM parse_ast('$prefix/bin/$tool arg', 'bash')
  WHERE type = 'command'
UNION ALL
SELECT '4 "$prefix/bin/$tool"', name FROM parse_ast('"$prefix/bin/$tool" arg', 'bash')
  WHERE type = 'command'
UNION ALL
SELECT '5 $''ansi''', name FROM parse_ast('$''ansi'' arg', 'bash') WHERE type = 'command'
ORDER BY src;
----
1 "$cmd"	$cmd
2 'ls'	ls
3 $prefix/bin/$tool	$prefix/bin/$tool
4 "$prefix/bin/$tool"	$prefix/bin/$tool
5 $'ansi'	$'ansi'

# The other command on each of those lines is unaffected and still names
# normally. This is why section 2 filters on `$`: a line holds two commands.
query TT
SELECT 'with $verbose' AS src, name FROM parse_ast('$verbose && echo x', 'bash')
  WHERE type = 'command' AND name NOT LIKE '$%'
UNION ALL
SELECT 'with $DEBUG', name FROM parse_ast('if $DEBUG; then :; fi', 'bash')
  WHERE type = 'command' AND name NOT LIKE '$%'
ORDER BY src;
----
with $DEBUG	:
with $verbose	echo

# =============================================================================
# 3. THE BOUNDARY: a command substitution is NOT named
# =============================================================================
# This section is the negative control. Without it, an implementation that took
# the written text of every name position would pass section 2 and be wrong.
#
# `$(which ls)` is a command_substitution. The command that runs is whatever
# `which ls` printed, and the inner command is already named `which` on its own
# row, so naming the outer one would invent a name for a node that has none.
# Quoting it changes nothing, which is why the second row is here: a string that
# HOLDS a substitution is the same fact.

query TTI
SELECT '1 $(which ls) -l' AS src, name, len(name) AS n
FROM parse_ast('$(which ls) -l', 'bash')
WHERE type = 'command' AND name = ''
UNION ALL
SELECT '2 "$(which ls)" -l', name, len(name)
FROM parse_ast('"$(which ls)" -l', 'bash')
WHERE type = 'command' AND name = ''
ORDER BY src;
----
1 $(which ls) -l	(empty)	0
2 "$(which ls)" -l	(empty)	0

# The inner command of each substitution is still named, so the fact is not lost.
query TT
SELECT '1 bare' AS which_row, name FROM parse_ast('$(which ls) -l', 'bash')
WHERE type = 'command' AND name <> ''
UNION ALL
SELECT '2 quoted', name FROM parse_ast('"$(which ls)" -l', 'bash')
WHERE type = 'command' AND name <> ''
ORDER BY which_row;
----
1 bare	which
2 quoted	which

# =============================================================================
# 4. The real fixture: every command in it is now named
# =============================================================================
# test/data/bash/simple.sh is in the conformance corpus, and its three
# `$system_ok` / `$verbose` / `$DEBUG` commands are what made the kit's
# CALL-NAMED check fail for bash.
#
# BOTH DENOMINATORS ARE ASSERTED, because they differ and confusing them is easy.
# The kit reports "3 of 95", counting commands across ALL THREE bash fixtures in
# the corpus. simple.sh alone holds 90. A single number here would not say which
# set it came from, and the first draft of this file asserted 95 against
# simple.sh alone and failed.

query II
SELECT count(*) FILTER (WHERE name = '' OR name IS NULL) AS unnamed,
       count(*)                                          AS total
FROM read_ast('test/data/bash/simple.sh')
WHERE type = 'command';
----
0	90

# The kit's own denominator: all three bash corpus fixtures.
query II
SELECT count(*) FILTER (WHERE name = '' OR name IS NULL) AS unnamed,
       count(*)                                          AS total
FROM read_ast(['test/data/unparse_leaf_text/sample.sh',
               'test/data/call_extraction/calls.sh',
               'test/data/bash/simple.sh'])
WHERE type = 'command';
----
0	95

# And the three are named as written, which is the behaviour change itself.
query TI
SELECT name, count(*) AS n
FROM read_ast('test/data/bash/simple.sh')
WHERE type = 'command' AND name LIKE '$%'
GROUP BY name ORDER BY name;
----
$DEBUG	1
$system_ok	1
$verbose	1
